Here's how to save/restore draft text using MAUI's app lifecycle events and `Preferences`:

**In your `App.xaml.cs`:**

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    public App()
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        MainPage = new AppShell();
    }

    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState activationState)
    {
        var window = base.CreateWindow(activationState);

        window.Deactivated += (s, e) =>
        {
            if (Shell.Current?.CurrentPage is NotePage page)
                page.SaveDraft();
        };

        window.Activated += (s, e) =>
        {
            if (Shell.Current?.CurrentPage is NotePage page)
                page.RestoreDraft();
        };

        return window;
    }
}
```

**In your `NotePage.xaml.cs`:**

```csharp
public partial class NotePage : ContentPage
{
    private const string DraftKey = "note_draft";

    public NotePage()
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        RestoreDraft();
    }

    public void SaveDraft()
    {
        Preferences.Set(DraftKey, NoteEditor.Text ?? string.Empty);
    }

    public void RestoreDraft()
    {
        if (Preferences.ContainsKey(DraftKey))
            NoteEditor.Text = Preferences.Get(DraftKey, string.Empty);
    }

    // Call this when the user explicitly saves/discards
    public void ClearDraft()
    {
        Preferences.Remove(DraftKey);
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- `Window.Deactivated` fires when the app goes to background
- `Window.Activated` fires when it returns to foreground
- `Preferences` persists small strings across app restarts (backed by `SharedPreferences` on Android, `NSUserDefaults` on iOS)
- For large text, use `SecureStorage` or write to `FileSystem.AppDataDirectory` instead
